58: \nL59: : the supreme council of the ancient Roman republic and empire\nL60: \nL61: 2\nL62: \nL63: : the hall or chamber in which a senate meets\nL64: \nL65: 3\nL66: \nL67: : a governing body of some universities charged with maintaining academic standards and regulations and usually composed of the principal or representative members of the faculty\nL68: ## Examples of senate in a Sentence\nL69: \nL70: the New York State Senate The Senate approved the bill. The Senate voted to repeal the tax cut.\nL71: \nL72: Recent Examples on the Web\nL73: \nL74: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【18†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL75: That election gave him a partial senate term representing much of North and West Tarrant County. — Fort Worth Star-Telegram, 26 Oct. 2026 Cornell University’s president and provost are scheduled to address the faculty senate and take questions before the debate begins, and the formal vote on the resolution is set to take place later this month. — ABC News, 14 Oct.\nL76: 2026 Real-life Texas is in the middle of a highly contentious senate race; TV Texas is a world with no Greg Abbott, nor a president who’s already a cautionary tale in what happens when pop culture and politics collide. — Alison Herman, Variety, 23 Sep. 2026 Each ban was a policy response to a design problem, and this time the people best positioned to solve it are not faculty senates but the technology leaders who decide what gets built, procured and deployed.\nL77: — Terry Oroszi, Forbes.com, 22 July 2026 【19†See All Example Sentences for senate】\nL78: ## Word History\nL79: \nL80: Etymology\nL81: \nL82: Middle English senat, from Anglo-French, from Latin senatus, from sen-, senex old, old man — more at 【20†senior】\nL83: \nL84: First Known Use\nL85: \nL86: 13th century, in the meaning defined at 【21†sense 1】\nL87: \nL88: Tim